𝗗𝗮𝘆 𝟭𝟬/𝟯𝟲𝟱 — 𝗗𝗲𝘃𝗢𝗽𝘀 𝗜𝗻𝘁𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗶𝗲𝘄 𝗖𝗵𝗮𝗹𝗹𝗲𝗻𝗴𝗲

🔥 𝗣𝗿𝗼𝗱𝘂𝗰𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻 𝗣𝗼𝗱 𝗶𝘀 𝗶𝗻 𝗖𝗿𝗮𝘀𝗵𝗟𝗼𝗼𝗽𝗕𝗮𝗰𝗸𝗢𝗳𝗳 — 𝗪𝗵𝗮𝘁 𝗪𝗶𝗹𝗹 𝗬𝗼𝘂 𝗗𝗼?

𝗜𝗻𝘁𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗶𝗲𝘄 𝗤𝘂𝗲𝘀𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻:

👉 “One of your production Kubernetes pods is continuously restarting and showing CrashLoopBackOff. How will you troubleshoot it?”

𝗜 𝘄𝗼𝘂𝗹𝗱𝗻'𝘁 𝗶𝗺𝗺𝗲𝗱𝗶𝗮𝘁𝗲𝗹𝘆 𝗿𝗲𝘀𝘁𝗮𝗿𝘁 𝘁𝗵𝗲 𝗽𝗼𝗱.

First, I would understand why the container is crashing.

🔍 𝗠𝘆 𝗧𝗿𝗼𝘂𝗯𝗹𝗲𝘀𝗵𝗼𝗼𝘁𝗶𝗻𝗴 𝗙𝗹𝗼𝘄

1️⃣ Check pod status

kubectl get pods -n <namespace>

2️⃣ Check pod details and events

kubectl describe pod <pod-name> -n <namespace>

Look for:

OOMKilled

FailedMount

ImagePull errors

Probe failures

Permission issues

Scheduling/events


3️⃣ Check application logs

kubectl logs <pod-name> -n <namespace>

If the container already restarted:

kubectl logs <pod-name> -n <namespace> --previous

This is especially important because the current container may have already lost the logs from the previous crash.

4️⃣ Check resource configuration

kubectl describe pod <pod-name> -n <namespace>

Look for CPU/memory requests and limits and check whether the container was terminated because of memory pressure.

5️⃣ Check probes

Review:

livenessProbe

readinessProbe

startupProbe


A badly configured probe can cause a healthy application to be restarted repeatedly.

6️⃣ Check recent changes

I would investigate:

👉 Recent deployment
👉 Image change
👉 ConfigMap/Secret change
👉 Environment variables
👉 Application configuration
👉 Dependency/database connectivity

🎯 𝗠𝘆 𝗧𝗿𝗼𝘂𝗯𝗹𝗲𝘀𝗵𝗼𝗼𝘁𝗶𝗻𝗴 𝗙𝗹𝗼𝘄:

Pod Status → Events → Previous Logs → Resources → Probes → Configuration → Recent Changes → Root Cause

After fixing the issue, I would verify the pod becomes stable and monitor the application.

𝗞𝗲𝘆 𝗹𝗲𝗮𝗿𝗻𝗶𝗻𝗴:

CrashLoopBackOff is not the root cause. It is a symptom.

The real job of a DevOps engineer is to find why the container keeps crashing.
